31	rmb-keep-flags	MAN:S8-67	RMB changes no flag (S8-67): the cell $01 gives a zero result with P = N V C set and Z clear, the masked P byte stays $C1 (a core that set Z reads $C3)
32	smb-keep-flags	MAN:S8-79	SMB changes no flag (S8-79): the cell $00 gives a negative result with P = V and C set and N and Z clear, the masked P byte stays $41 (a core that set N reads $C1)
33	rmb-neighbours	MAN:S8-67	RMB changes only its own zero-page cell (S8-67): the cells before and after it hold $5A and $3C and read $5A EOR $3C = $66 after RMB2 on the cell between them
34	smb-neighbours	MAN:S8-79	SMB changes only its own zero-page cell (S8-79): the cells before and after it hold $5A and $3C and read $66 after SMB5 on the cell between them
35	tma-mpr7-reset	MAN:H8-8	A reset sets MPR7 to $00 (H8-8, H8-18): TMA with the operand $80 (S8-101) reads $00 into A, which held $FF
36	tma-mpr0	MAN:S8-101	TMA with the one-hot operand $01 reads MPR0 into A (S8-101): after TAM wrote the seven distinct values $F8 $FB $FA $33 $44 $55 $66 to MPR0 to MPR6 (S8-90) it reads $F8
37	tma-mpr1	MAN:S8-101	TMA with the one-hot operand $02 reads MPR1 into A (S8-101): after TAM wrote the seven distinct values $F8 $FB $FA $33 $44 $55 $66 to MPR0 to MPR6 (S8-90) it reads $FB
38	tma-mpr2	MAN:S8-101	TMA with the one-hot operand $04 reads MPR2 into A (S8-101): after TAM wrote the seven distinct values $F8 $FB $FA $33 $44 $55 $66 to MPR0 to MPR6 (S8-90) it reads $FA
39	tma-mpr3	MAN:S8-101	TMA with the one-hot operand $08 reads MPR3 into A (S8-101): after TAM wrote the seven distinct values $F8 $FB $FA $33 $44 $55 $66 to MPR0 to MPR6 (S8-90) it reads $33
40	tma-mpr4	MAN:S8-101	TMA with the one-hot operand $10 reads MPR4 into A (S8-101): after TAM wrote the seven distinct values $F8 $FB $FA $33 $44 $55 $66 to MPR0 to MPR6 (S8-90) it reads $44
41	tma-mpr5	MAN:S8-101	TMA with the one-hot operand $20 reads MPR5 into A (S8-101): after TAM wrote the seven distinct values $F8 $FB $FA $33 $44 $55 $66 to MPR0 to MPR6 (S8-90) it reads $55
42	tma-mpr6	MAN:S8-101	TMA with the one-hot operand $40 reads MPR6 into A (S8-101): after TAM wrote the seven distinct values $F8 $FB $FA $33 $44 $55 $66 to MPR0 to MPR6 (S8-90) it reads $66
43	tam-xlate-mpr0	MAN:S8-90	TAM with the operand $01 loads MPR0 (S8-90): MPR0 = $F8 maps logical $0000 to work RAM, so a read there returns the marker $A7 written at $2000
44	tam-xlate-mpr3	MAN:S8-90	TAM with the operand $08 loads MPR3 (S8-90): MPR3 = $F8 maps logical $6000 to work RAM, so a read there returns the marker $A7
45	tam-xlate-mpr4	MAN:S8-90	TAM with the operand $10 loads MPR4 (S8-90): MPR4 = $F8 maps logical $8000 to work RAM, so a read there returns the marker $A7
46	tam-xlate-mpr5	MAN:S8-90	TAM with the operand $20 loads MPR5 (S8-90): MPR5 = $F8 maps logical $A000 to work RAM, so a read there returns the marker $A7
47	tam-xlate-mpr6	MAN:S8-90	TAM with the operand $40 loads MPR6 (S8-90): MPR6 = $F8 maps logical $C000 to work RAM, so a read there returns the marker $A7
48	tam-keep-flags	MAN:S8-90	TAM changes no flag (S8-90): P = V and C set with N and Z clear, A = $80, the masked P byte stays $41 (a core that set N reads $C1)
49	tma-keep-flags-n	MAN:S8-101	TMA changes no flag (S8-101): MPR3 holds $80, P = V and C set with N and Z clear, the masked P byte stays $41 after the read of a byte with bit 7 set
50	tma-keep-flags-z	MAN:S8-101	TMA changes no flag (S8-101): MPR7 holds $00, P = N V and C set with Z clear, the masked P byte stays $C1 after the read of a zero byte
51	csh-length	MAN:H8-25	CSH is one byte (H8-25 map, no software-manual page): the instruction after it runs, so INX after CSH leaves X = $01
52	csl-length	MAN:H8-25	CSL is one byte (H8-25 map, no software-manual page): the instruction after it runs, so INX after CSL leaves X = $01
53	csh-csl-pair	MAN:H8-25	CSH then CSL execute and execution goes on at the next byte each time: two INX, one after each, leave X = $02
54	st0-bypass	GUEST:ram-before-equals-ram-after	ST0 writes physical $1FE000 and does not consult the MPRs (H8-8 Table 2-2-3, S8-80, D-106): with MPR0 mapped to work RAM, RAM[0] still holds the $5A the guest wrote, read at logical $0000
55	st1-bypass	GUEST:ram-before-equals-ram-after	ST1 writes physical $1FE002 and does not consult the MPRs (H8-8 Table 2-2-3, S8-81, D-106): with MPR0 mapped to work RAM, RAM[2] still holds the $5B the guest wrote
56	st2-bypass	GUEST:ram-before-equals-ram-after	ST2 writes physical $1FE003 and does not consult the MPRs (H8-8 Table 2-2-3, S8-82, D-106): with MPR0 mapped to work RAM, RAM[3] still holds the $5C the guest wrote
57	st-lengths	MAN:S8-80	ST0, ST1 and ST2 are two bytes each (S8-80 to S8-82): an INX after each runs three times, and the operand $E8 (the INX opcode) is never executed, so X reads $03
58	st-keep-flags	MAN:S8-80	ST0 changes no flag (flag row of S8-80, S8-81, S8-82 is T = 0 and dashes): P = N V C set and Z clear, the operand $00 does not set Z, the masked P byte stays $C1
59	ora-modes	MAN:S8-58	ORA (zz,X), zz, (zz), zz,X, abs,Y and abs,X of the operand cells (see the file header): A = $00 ORed with the six cells and with zz again gives $6F (S8-58): the cells $04, $01, $08, $02, $40 and $20 hold no bit $10
60	and-modes	MAN:S8-18	AND (zz,X), (zz),Y, (zz), zz,X, abs,Y and abs,X with A = $3F each time (S8-18): the ORed results are $04, $10, $08, $02, $00 and $20, so $3E
61	eor-modes	MAN:S8-47	EOR (zz,X), zz, (zz),Y, (zz), zz,X and abs,Y then zz again from A = $FF (S8-47): $FF EOR $5F EOR $01 gives $A1
62	adc-modes	MAN:S8-17	ADC (zz),Y, (zz) and (zz) again with C clear from A = $00 (S8-17): $10 + $08 + $08 gives $20
63	sbc-modes	MAN:S8-74	SBC (zz,X), zz, (zz),Y, (zz), zz,X, abs,Y and abs,X with C set from A = $FF (S8-74): $FF minus $04, $01, $10, $08, $02, $40 and $20 gives $80
64	cmp-modes	MAN:S8-41	CMP (zz,X), zz, (zz),Y, (zz), abs,Y and abs,X with A equal to the cell (S8-41): Z and C set each time, so the ORed cell values give $7D
